/// @defgroup app app
/// The program put together: App owns the main thread's systems (window,
/// renderer, display) and starts the logic thread, where Game owns the logic thread's (jobs, saves,
/// the simulation, the logic, the tick clock). The only module that uses every other one.
///
/// @par Running the game
/// `./dev run [--debug] [-- <game flags>]` runs the native preset (`--preset` picks another). The game's flags:
/// - `--stress`: the stress arena, about 100,000 instances. `--painted`: the still painted test scene. `--cells`: a map
///   cell grid, to judge the mips as the view zooms. `--world`: the world map, 20 screens across, its painted terrain and
///   the travellers between its cities (`game/scenes/world.hpp`). `--<name>` for each scene in `art/scenes.toml` the game
///   knows.
/// - `--quit-after SECONDS`: quit by itself.
/// - `--fail-frames` (debug and releasedbg builds): every frame fails to acquire, so the game stops after 3 seconds and
///   120 frames with the dialog for a graphics card that stopped drawing, and the exit status 1.
/// - `--zoom VALUE`: every scene starts at this zoom, from 0.25 (the farthest) to 2 (the closest), for
///   `profile -- --cast-swords --zoom 2`.
/// - `--burst`: F7's burst once a second, for `profile -- --burst`.
/// - `--debug-sight`: every tile of the world map in sight from the start, unexplored land staying under cloud (F3 flips
///   it; the switch snaps the fade, explored tiles still ease in as they are explored, and the host's sight and explored
///   tiles are untouched).
/// - `--cast-swords`: the flying swords beside the player 4 seconds after the last cast or as soon after as they are ready
///   (about every 6 seconds), the fire bolt topping the Qi up first, for `profile -- --cast-swords`, with each cast's
///   latency to its first drawn sword in the log file at debug level.
/// - `--workers N`: pin the job system to N workers, 1 to 64. `--unpaced`: present immediate before mailbox, so the
///   display's rate doesn't cap a measurement.
/// - `--camera-path` (for `--world`): 2 seconds after the scene runs, the camera follows a fixed path (`app/camera_path.hpp`,
///   which `app::CameraControl` moves by: follow the cultivator walking east by a scripted Move in steps of 0.5 s (standing
///   0.1 s, then walking 0.4 s: eight presses, each from a standing cultivator), pan 3 screens, zoom in to 2 and out to
///   0.25, the widest view over the busiest region, zoom 0.6 with labels) and the game quits after it, logging
///   `camera path: <segment>` at each segment's start (also a Tracy message) and
///   `input latency: <ms> ms from a Move press to its first drawn frame` for each press.
/// - `--travel-share PER_MILLE`: the world map's travel share (`world.yaml`'s `share_per_mille`) set to this, 0 to 1,000,
///   before the game starts (`game::SetTravelShare`, so the data's hash follows it), for `measure`'s world run.
/// - `--month-step-at SECONDS` (with `--camera-path`): one month passes (`core::ActionKind::AdvanceCalendar`, as M sends
///   it) at that second of the path, logging `camera path: month step` (also a Tracy message; `app::PathMonthStep`,
///   `app::MonthStepAtOf`), for `measure`'s world run.
/// - `--fullscreen`: this run fullscreen at the display's native size, whatever `settings.yaml` says; the log names the
///   display mode as `display: WxH at N Hz (name)`, again whenever the window lands on another display or its mode
///   changes (a compositor may place a new window elsewhere than SDL opened it).
///
/// @par Default bindings (`app/bindings.cpp`)
/// - WASD, the arrows, the d-pad or the left stick walk the cultivator (pan the still scenes; walking onto a scene's
///   glowing portal goes to the next walkable scene: arena, stress, world, then the arena again).
/// - In the arena a left click casts the generator, a right click the spender, Space the movement and 1 to 5 the specials
///   and ultimate (Alt and a left click selects). Elsewhere a left click selects, except that on `world` one on open
///   ground walks the cultivator there and lets go of the selection.
/// - Outside combat the camera pans (and drags) freely: I, J, K, L or the right stick pan it at one screen a second, so
///   does the pointer within 2% of the window's edge (`edge_pan: false` in `settings.yaml` turns that off), the middle
///   button (and the right, outside combat) drags it.
/// - In `world` the camera follows the cultivator loosely (it moves only when the foot leaves a centred box half the
///   view's width and height, by the overshoot); panning frees it until C, the right stick's click or a middle click that
///   drags nothing snaps it back to the cultivator at the centre (in a still scene, back to its start; combat scenes
///   always follow the cultivator exactly).
/// - The wheel, the shoulder buttons or `-` and `=` (by their place on the keyboard) zoom. Escape quits. P or the
///   gamepad's Back pauses the world (the date line's click too).
/// - On `world`, T or the gamepad's Y shows the target cursor on the cultivator's tile (the same again hides it; it hides
///   too when a click walks or selects). While it shows, the movement keys, the d-pad or the left stick step it a tile at
///   a time (held: after 0.3 s, then every 0.15 s) instead of walking the cultivator, and Enter or the gamepad's A walks
///   there, as a click does (`app::TargetCursor`).
///
/// @par Debug keys
/// F2 switches to the next scene (arena, stress, painted, cells, world; the portals skip the still ones). F3 puts every
/// tile of the world map in sight (unexplored land stays under cloud) or stops. M lets a month pass at once (the world
/// clock jumps to the next month's first tick). F5 saves, F9 loads, F6 starts or finishes recording a replay, F10 plays or
/// stops it; saves and the replay go to the per-user data folder's `saves/`. F7 hits every fox thousands of times (the
/// damage numbers' stress). F8 hides and shows the arena's showcase seals.
///
/// @par Data reload
/// Debug builds reload `assets/data/` (the source tree's; all but `world.yaml`, `names.yaml` and `strings.yaml`, read once
/// at start) within half a second of an edit; a broken edit is logged with its line and the old data stays.
